---
import StandardPageLayout from "../../layouts/StandardPageLayout.astro";
import PageToc from "../../components/PageToc.astro";
import "../../styles/standards-poster.css";
import "../../styles/extended.css";
import metadata from "../../data/standards/hgb/hgb330.json";
---

<StandardPageLayout
  code={metadata.code}
  standardType={metadata.framework}
  title={metadata.title}
  description={metadata.summary}
  issued={metadata.issued}
  latestAmendment={metadata.latestAmendment}
  summary={metadata.summary}
  subtitle={metadata.nativeTitle}
  covers={metadata.covers}
>
  <Fragment slot="toc">
    <PageToc
      items={[
        { id: "s1", label: "Conceptual Framework Linkage" },
        { id: "s2", label: "Scope" },
        { id: "s3", label: "What §330 Actually Does — a Delegation, Not a Rule" },
        { id: "s4", label: "Why Delegate at All — the Practical Case for Regulatory Flexibility" },
        { id: "s5", label: "Worked Example — Where a Formblatt Regulation Would Matter" },
        { id: "s6", label: "Critical Judgments &amp; Practical Difficulties" },
        { id: "s7", label: "Relationship to IFRS" },
        { id: "s8", label: "Related Provisions — Cross-Reference Map" },
        { id: "s9", label: "Quick-Reference Glossary" }
      ]}
    />
  </Fragment>
  

  <div class="wrap">
<!-- ============ SECTION 1 ============ -->
  
  <div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci">
      <div class="callout info">A DELIBERATELY NARROW POSTER: §330 is a single, purely enabling provision — it does not itself prescribe any substantive accounting rule. It authorises the federal government to issue detailed regulations (Rechtsverordnungen) filling in technical specifics this Book has left to delegated rulemaking, particularly for specialised industries. Given its narrow legal text, this poster is scaled accordingly rather than padded to match the anchor posters.</div>

  
      <div class="ctitle ci">A single hinge connecting primary law to detailed, specialised technical rules</div>
      <div class="principle">Nearly everything this poster series has covered lives directly in HGB's own statutory text. §330 is different in kind: it is the LEGAL HINGE authorising the federal government to issue separate, delegated regulations (Rechtsverordnungen) — most notably prescribing detailed Formblätter (standard forms) for the Bilanz and GuV of certain specialised company types, particularly where Book 3's own general presentation rules don't naturally fit an industry's genuine operational reality.</div>
      <div class="elabel">HOW §330 MAPS TO GERMAN ACCOUNTING PRINCIPLES</div>
      <div class="check-list">
        <span><strong>Klarer Einblick, for specialised industries</strong> — the SAME underlying transparency objective this whole series has documented, achieved here through TAILORED, industry-specific presentation formats rather than a one-size-fits-all approach</span>
        <span><strong>Legislative efficiency</strong> — highly technical, frequently-updated presentation details are better suited to delegated regulation than to primary statute, which requires the full parliamentary process to amend</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="card cs">
      <div class="ctitle cs">What §330 does, at a glance</div>
      <div class="principle">§330 authorises the competent federal ministry to issue regulations PRESCRIBING detailed standard forms and further supplementary technical rules for the Bilanz and GuV of certain Kapitalgesellschaften — most notably those in specialised sectors (credit institutions, insurance undertakings, and comparable industries) whose operational realities the GENERAL §266/§275 formats established earlier in this series don't naturally accommodate.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 2 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2">
      <div class="ctitle cw">Primarily specialised-industry Kapitalgesellschaften</div>
      <div class="principle">§330's practical significance concentrates on Kapitalgesellschaften in specialised sectors — credit institutions, insurance undertakings, and comparable industries covered by Book 3, Section 4's own further supplementary provisions — whose genuine business structure the GENERAL Bilanz/GuV formats this series has documented in detail were never designed to accommodate cleanly.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 3 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>What §330 Actually Does — a Delegation, Not a Rule</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2">
      <div class="ctitle cd">Empowering, not itself prescribing</div>
      <div class="text-supporting">§330 does not itself set out a Formblatt, a measurement rule, or a disclosure requirement — it EMPOWERS the responsible federal authority to do so through separate, delegated regulations. This is a genuinely different KIND of provision than nearly everything else this poster series has covered: rather than a substantive rule readers apply directly, §330 is the constitutional-style hinge explaining WHERE certain specialised technical detail actually comes from, even though it doesn't appear in HGB's own text.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 4 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>Why Delegate at All — the Practical Case for Regulatory Flexibility</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2">
      <div class="ctitle cw">Technical detail that changes faster than primary legislation reasonably can</div>
      <div class="text-supporting">Highly specific, industry-tailored presentation formats — particularly for credit institutions and insurers, whose regulatory and operational environment evolves considerably faster than general commercial law — are genuinely better suited to DELEGATED regulation than to primary statute. Amending a Rechtsverordnung is procedurally faster and more technically flexible than amending HGB itself through the full parliamentary legislative process, allowing specialised presentation rules to be kept reasonably current without requiring Book 3's own core text to be constantly revised.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 5 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>Worked Example — Where a Formblatt Regulation Would Matter</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cs span2">
      <div class="ctitle cs">A specialised balance sheet structure the general format was never built for</div>
      <div class="worked">
        <div class="wk-title">Example 1: A credit institution's balance sheet structure</div>
        <div class="wk-body">A licensed credit institution's balance sheet is dominated by items — customer deposits, interbank lending, loan portfolios, trading securities — that the GENERAL §266 asset/liability categories established earlier in this series (Anlagevermögen/Umlaufvermögen, ordinary Verbindlichkeiten) describe only awkwardly, if at all.</div>
        <div class="wk-answer">Rather than forcing the credit institution into the GENERAL Kapitalgesellschaft format, a Formblatt regulation issued under the §330 authorisation prescribes a SPECIALISED, industry-tailored Bilanz and GuV structure specifically fitted to a bank's own genuine balance sheet composition — the practical mechanism §330 exists to enable, even though the SPECIFIC form itself lives in a separate regulation rather than in HGB's own statutory text.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 6 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>Critical Judgments &amp; Practical Difficulties</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cn span2">
      <div class="ctitle cn">Where judgement matters most</div>
      <div class="two-col">
        <div>
          <div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGEMENTS</div>
          <div class="dot-list">
            <span>Whether a specific industry's presentation needs genuinely warrant a specialised Formblatt, versus simply applying the general format with the flexibility §265(4) already provides</span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div>
          <div class="elabel">PRACTICAL DIFFICULTIES</div>
          <div class="dot-list">
            <span>Locating the actual, current delegated regulation itself — since it exists SEPARATELY from HGB's own statutory text, a practitioner working only from HGB directly would not find the specific Formblatt content there at all</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 7 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>Relationship to IFRS</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci span2">
      <div class="ctitle ci">A structural difference in how specialised presentation gets handled</div>
      <div class="text-supporting">IFRS has no direct equivalent delegation mechanism of this kind — where a specialised industry's presentation needs diverge from IAS 1's general minimum-line-item approach, IFRS typically addresses this through INDUSTRY-SPECIFIC STANDARDS (e.g. IFRS 17 for insurance contracts) issued by the IASB itself through its own standard-setting process, rather than through a separate, government-delegated regulatory mechanism sitting outside the core standards altogether. Both approaches solve the SAME underlying problem — general formats don't fit every industry — through genuinely different institutional architectures.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 8 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Related Provisions — Cross-Reference Map</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2">
      <div class="ctitle cd">How §330 connects across HGB Book 3</div>
      <div class="td-table">
        <div class="th cols-08-2"><span>Provision</span><span>Nature of the connection</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-08-2"><span>§266, §275</span><span>The general Bilanz/GuV formats a §330 Formblatt regulation supplants for specific specialised industries</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt cols-08-2"><span>§265(4)</span><span>The narrower, in-statute format-adaptation flexibility already available before resorting to a full separate regulation</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-08-2"><span>Book 3, Section 4</span><span>The credit institution/insurance-specific statutory provisions §330's delegated regulations most commonly supplement</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt cols-08-2"><span>§331 et seq.</span><span>The NEXT Titel in this series — criminal and administrative fine provisions — closing out Book 3, Section 2 entirely</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 9 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="three-col">
    <div class="card cn">
      <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Verordnungsermächtigung</div><div class="glossary-def">Regulatory authorization — a statutory provision empowering the executive to issue delegated regulations on a specific technical topic.</div></div>
      <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Rechtsverordnung</div><div class="glossary-def">A delegated regulation issued by the executive under statutory authorization, without requiring the full parliamentary legislative process.</div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="card cn">
      <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Formblatt</div><div class="glossary-def">Standard form — a prescribed, industry-tailored Bilanz/GuV presentation format issued under a §330 regulation.</div></div>
      <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Ermächtigungsnorm</div><div class="glossary-def">An enabling provision — the general legal category §330 belongs to, granting delegated rulemaking power rather than stating a substantive rule itself.</div></div>
    </div>
  </div>
  </div>
</StandardPageLayout>
